<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e8f3f8;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Let's Dress for a Dance. – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Traditional Style of Dress</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Let's Dress for a Dance.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Collaborate with peers to plan a simple presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> 
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Speaking: Can provide oral instructions to a peer for creating traditional clothing (e.g., 'First, cut the paper. Next, fold it. Then, draw the pollera.').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can collaborate with peers to create a presentation about the cultural importance of traditional clothing with simple reasons (e.g., 'People wear polleras at festivals.').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can describe cultural clothing with peers to enhance group understanding (e.g., 'People wear traditional clothing to celebrations.').</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e8f3f8; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e8f3f8; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e8f3f8; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or large pictures of people wearing different Panamanian traditional clothing (e.g., pollera, montuno). Students chorally repeat the names of the clothing items and point to details. The teacher asks simple questions like, "What is she wearing?" or "Is it pretty?". This activates prior vocabulary from Lessons 1 and 2 and sets the stage for describing. </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models describing one of the traditional outfits using present continuous and present simple. For example, pointing to a pollera: "She is wearing a beautiful pollera. It is very festive. Women wear this dress at festivals and celebrations." The teacher clearly models the pronunciation of /w/ in 'wearing' and /t/ in 'traditional'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding with at least two Concept Checking Questions:
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is she wearing a modern dress or traditional clothing?" (Checks 'traditional clothing' vs. 'modern')</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do people wear polleras for sleeping or for a celebration?" (Checks 'celebration', 'wear')</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Traditional Style of Dress".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated dialogue between two friends planning what to wear for a cultural event. The dialogue is presented on the whiteboard or through a projector.
                    <br><em>Example Dialogue:</em>
                    <br>Maria: "What are you wearing for the community event?"
                    <br>Sofia: "I am wearing my traditional pollera! My mom is sewing some pretty jewelry on it. It is very important."
                    <br>Maria: "Oh, that's beautiful attire! People admire it during the celebration. Are you going to showcase it for a dance?"
                    <br>Sofia: "Yes! It represents our culture. What about you?"
                    <br>Maria: "I'm wearing a modern dress, but I admire your traditional clothing."
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing traditional clothing: 'She is wearing a pollera.' Explaining cultural significance: 'Polleras are worn at festivals.' Discussing traditions: Describing different traditional clothing designs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to or read the dialogue.
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What are Maria and Sofia talking about?" "Where are they going?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "Sofia is wearing a modern dress." (False) "Maria admires Sofia's pollera." (True) "Sofia's mom is folding jewelry." (False, she is sewing)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using present continuous ('She is wearing...'), WH-questions ('What are you wearing?'), and present simple ('Women wear this dress at festivals.'). Vocabulary includes 'attire', 'celebration', 'event', 'community', 'traditional clothing', 'jewelry', 'showcase', 'represent', 'explain', 'admire', 'dress', 'use', 'wear', 'fold', 'sew', 'festive', 'pretty', 'beautiful', 'cultural', 'important', 'traditional', 'old-fashioned', 'modern', 'during', 'at', 'in'. Pronunciation practice for /w/ in 'wearing' and /t/ in 'these', and blending /dr/ /ɛs/ for 'dress' and /pr-ɪ-t-i/ for 'pretty'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice/Sentence Builders:</b> The teacher writes sentence starters on the board related to describing clothing. Students complete the sentences orally. E.g., "She is _______ (wear) a _______ (beautiful/festive) _______ (pollera/attire)." "People _______ (wear/use) traditional clothing for a _______ (celebration/event)." The teacher guides students to produce full sentences, focusing on the correct verb forms and adjective placement.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Substitution Drill (Think-Pair-Share):</b> The teacher provides a picture of traditional clothing and a sentence frame like, "They are wearing _______. They wear it for _______." Students work in pairs to substitute words to create new sentences. E.g., (picture of montuno) "They are wearing a montuno. They wear it for folk dances." (picture of pollera) "She is wearing a pollera. She wears it at festivals." The teacher encourages using different vocabulary words such as 'pretty', 'important', 'cultural', 'community'.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction / Describe and Guess:</b> Students work in pairs. Each student receives a picture of a different piece of traditional Panamanian clothing (e.g., a short pollera, a montuno, indigenous attire). Without showing the picture, Student A describes the clothing item to Student B using the target grammar and vocabulary (e.g., "She is wearing a dress. It is white and colorful. People wear it during celebrations. It is beautiful traditional clothing."). Student B listens and guesses what it is. Then they switch roles. Teacher monitors for pronunciation and correct use of target language.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Traditional Style of Dress".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Planning for a Simple Presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in small groups of 3-4. Each group chooses one traditional Panamanian outfit (e.g., pollera, montuno) to focus on for a "Let's Dress for a Dance" presentation.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Their task is to collaboratively plan a very simple 1-2 minute oral presentation about their chosen attire. They must discuss and decide:
                            <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What is it called? (e.g., "This is a pollera.")</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What does it look like? (e.g., "It is a beautiful dress. She is wearing pretty jewelry.")</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">When do people wear it? (e.g., "People wear it at festivals and celebrations.")</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Why is it important? (e.g., "It represents our community. It is cultural and important.")</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They must also practice giving simple oral instructions related to making or wearing the clothing, for instance, imagining they are telling someone how to prepare a paper doll with the outfit: "First, cut the paper. Next, fold the dress. Then, draw the pollera." (Learning Outcome 1 focus)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, offering support, encouraging collaboration, and prompting students with WH-questions like "What are they wearing?" or "Why do people wear it?". The focus is on fluent communication and successful task completion within the group.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation using a simple checklist for spoken contributions and group collaboration. Peers will also provide simple feedback.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group briefly presents their plan (1-2 minutes) to another group or the whole class. They describe their chosen traditional clothing and explain its importance using simple reasons, incorporating some oral instructions.
                    <br><em>Example:</em> "This is a pollera. It is beautiful attire. Women wear it during important events like celebrations. First, you draw the top. Next, you draw the skirt."
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear sounds /w/ and /t/, and blends like /dr/ /ɛs/ and /pr-ɪ-t-i/.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> (attire, celebration, traditional clothing, pretty, important, use, wear, fold, sew, explain, represent)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of descriptions:</b> Uses present continuous and present simple to describe and explain.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Participates and listens to peers during planning and presentation.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short reflection on a sticky note.
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can describe traditional clothing to my friend. (Yes / A little / No)"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can say why traditional clothing is important. (Yes / A little / No)"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy today when talking about traditional clothing with your group?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult for you today, and why?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The collaborative planning and short presentations worked well, allowing students to actively use the target language. Most students were able to describe the clothing and give simple reasons for its importance. Some struggled with sentence structure for giving instructions, requiring more modeling for phrases like "First, Next, Then." Pacing was good, but some groups needed more time for rehearsing their mini-presentations. Next time, provide sentence frames specifically for instructions to scaffold this further. The blend of simple onsets and rhymes for pronunciation was effective.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 3, students practiced speaking about traditional clothing and planning a presentation. Lesson 4 will focus on Writing Composition, where students will use the descriptions and reasons they discussed today to write simple sentences and short paragraphs about traditional Panamanian attire and its cultural importance, building directly on the oral practice.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>